  "# Kotoba — Y Combinator application draft (F26 late / W27)\n\n**Status:** draft, ready to paste except OWNER-FILL fields\n**As-of:** 2026-08-13\n**Form:** live YC apply form (company / progress / idea / equity / video)\n**Submit:** owner only. Do not submit until the 1-minute video URL exists.\n**Legal entity:** not formed yet. Owner will form a Delaware C-Corp later (ADR-2608139970).\n\nDo not paste sister-product metrics as Kotoba traction. Verified external revenue of the language = 0.\n\n---\n\n## Founders\n\nTwo founders: Jun Kawasaki (Tokyo; language/compiler) and Ichihara (marketing, sales, bizdev; invited, profile still incomplete at i@awai.network). Known more than 15 years; university senpai; ran a company together when Jun was 20. Planned equity Jun 90% / Ichihara 10%.\n\nHow long have the founders known one another?\nIchihara is my university senpai. We have known each other for more than 15 years and have met in person. We ran a company together when I was 20. Ichihara is co-founder on this application (invite: i@awai.network; profile still incomplete).\n\nWho writes code, or does other technical work on your product? Was any of it done by a non-founder?\nJun Kawasaki writes the language, compiler (Wasm + native x86-64/AArch64), and the public site. AI coding tools (Cursor, Claude) are used as editors. No outsourced engineering team. Ichihara is co-founder for marketing, sales, and bizdev, not compiler authorship.\n\nAre you looking for a cofounder?\nNo. Ichihara is already invited as co-founder on this application. Not looking for an additional cofounder through YC matching.\n\nFounder bio (paste into the founder profile):\nI am the technical founder in Tokyo (CEO/CTO, 90%). Ichihara is co-founder for marketing, sales, and bizdev (10%); profile still incomplete. I designed Kotoba, a capability-safe language for programs written by AI, and I wrote the compiler that emits WebAssembly and native x86-64/AArch64 machine code. I use it in production: 33 inference decision cores on our own fleet compile through that path to machine code (ADR-2608061000). I also operate sister products (kotobase.net, itonami.cloud) under separate companies; those are not this application and have $0 verified external revenue. Before Kotoba I built Gftd Japan as a security-focused operating company. At DEF CON 33 (Las Vegas, August 2025) I placed 2nd among one-person teams and 9th overall in the Car Hacking Village CTF (about 40 teams; https://prtimes.jp/main/html/rd/p/000000005.000129278.html). The through-line is the same: software should not receive ambient authority just because it compiled.\n\nOWNER-FILL: education, age, phone, LinkedIn, Twitter/X, personal email if not hello@gftd.co.jp, GitHub: https://github.com/com-junkawasaki (correct). Do not use github.com/junkawasaki on the YC bio..\n\nPlease tell us about a time you most successfully hacked some (non-computer) system to your advantage.\nOWNER-FILL. Do not invent. A usable shape: one system, one constraint, one action, one concrete outcome. Not a school grade appeal. Not a programming story.\n\n---\n\n## Founder video\n\nPlease record a one minute video introducing the founder(s).\nOWNER-FILL URL (YouTube unlisted). No demo, no slides, no reading a script. Phone or Zoom is enough.\n\nTalking points (do not read aloud):\n- 0:00 I am Jun Kawasaki. This is Ichihara, co-founder. I built Kotoba; Ichihara does marketing, sales, and bizdev.\n- 0:08 AI now writes most new software, but it runs with the same authority as a trusted human.\n- 0:20 Kotoba is a language where a program cannot use the network, disk, or money unless you grant a typed capability.\n- 0:35 The compiler already emits Wasm and native machine code. We use it ourselves on production inference cores.\n- 0:48 No external customers yet. Two founders, 15+ years. Applying so we can sell this as one product, not a pile of infrastructure.\n\nDemo video (optional, separate field): skip until a 60-second compile-and-deny-capability screencast exists. Do not substitute the founder video with a screencast.\n\n---\n\n## Company\n\nCompany name: Kotoba\n\nDescribe what your company does in 50 characters or less:\nLanguage + deploy for untrusted AI code\n(39 characters. The company is Deno-shaped: language plus hosted deploy. Not a language-only research apply.)\n\nCompany URL: https://kotoba-lang.org\n\nProduct link: https://github.com/kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang\nOrgs (public, already shipped): https://github.com/kotoba-lang , https://github.com/cloud-itonami , https://github.com/net-kotobase\nSite: https://kotoba-lang.org\n\nLogin credentials: none. Public site.\n\nWhat is your company going to make?\nKotoba is a capability-safe language with a deploy plane in the same product, the way Deno is a runtime plus Deno Deploy. AI-written programs get no network, disk, or money unless you grant a typed capability. The compiler already emits WebAssembly and native x86-64/ARM. We already run 33 of our own inference cores (murakumo) through that compiler. That is dogfood of the deploy path, not a second company. The missing paid face is hosted admission: submit code, grant capabilities, run, get a signed receipt. First external customers are teams that already let agents write production software.\n\nWhere do you live now, and where would the company be based after YC?\nTokyo, Japan / San Francisco, United States\n\nExplain your decision regarding location.\nJun Kawasaki lives in Tokyo. The batch is in-person in San Francisco, so the company will be there for those three months. After the batch, SF remains the commercial base; Tokyo remains where we build. Jun will relocate for the batch. Ichihara's batch location will be on their founder profile (still incomplete).\n\nBatch preference:\nFall 2026 (late application). Winter 2027 is acceptable if review slips. Do not wait on incorporation.\n\n---\n\n## Progress\n\nHow far along are you?\nWorking compiler and public language site. Compiles to WebAssembly and to native x86-64 and AArch64. We dogfood the deploy path: 33 production inference cores on murakumo compile to machine code through this compiler. No public billed deploy yet. No external language customers. No revenue. The next ship is hosted admission with capability grants, not more language surface.\n\nHow long have each of you been working on this? How much of that has been full-time?\nFull-time on Kotoba and its compiler through 2026. I do not have a clean earlier start date to claim, so I am not inventing one. Sister products and the Japanese operating company are older; they are background, not this company's age.\n\nWhat tech stack are you using?\nKotoba (.kotoba source). Compiler written in portable Clojure/ClojureScript. Backends: Wasm / Wasm Component, native x86-64, native AArch64. Site on Cloudflare. AI editors: Cursor and Claude. Inference for our own tools: self-hosted murakumo-main. No JVM in the shipped language runtime.\n\nOptional coding-agent session: skip unless owner exports a real session they want partners to read.\n\nAre people using your product?\nYes, internally. No, not as paying external language users.\n\nHow many active users or customers do you have? How many are paying?\nExternal paying customers of Kotoba the language: 0. Internal production use: our own inference fleet (33 compiled cores). Public site exists; we do not have a measured external developer-user count we trust, so we are not inventing one.\n\nWhen will you have a version people can use?\nThe compiler and site are usable now. A hosted runtime with billed capability grants is the missing productized face. That is the first thing to ship if accepted.\n\nDo you have revenue?\nNo.\n\nHow much revenue? / Where does your revenue come from?\n$0. Do not list sister-product traffic or historical ad cents as Kotoba revenue.\n\nPrevious YC application / pivot:\nFirst application. No prior YC batch.\n\nIncubator / accelerator:\nNone.\n\n---\n\n## Idea\n\nWhy did you pick this idea? Domain expertise? How do you know people need it?\nI run a company where AI agents write most of the new code. The remaining failure is not syntax. It is ambient authority: generated code that can reach the network, secrets, and money because the language allowed it. Process sandboxes still contain a full OS. I built Kotoba because I needed a language that cannot express forbidden effects. The need is from our own production, not from a TAM slide. Domain expertise: at DEF CON 33 I competed as a one-person team in the Car Hacking Village CTF and placed 2nd among one-person teams and 9th overall.\n\nWho are your competitors? What do you understand that they don't?\nDeno and Deno Deploy, Cloudflare Workers, E2B, Modal, gVisor, Firecracker. They sandbox a process that still speaks JavaScript or Python with ambient authority. We change the language so forbidden effects cannot be expressed, then host that language the way Deno hosts JavaScript. WASM Component Model is plumbing, not a product. seL4 and Capsicum are capability OSes without a language AI agents actually write. kotobase is not a competitor; it is our own storage kit. itonami is not this company.\n\nHow do or will you make money? How much could you make?\nDeno model. The language can be open source. The company sells hosted deploy. Usage customers pay per invocation and per capability grant, like Deno Deploy. Team customers pay for policy: which capabilities an agent may hold, with a signed receipt of what ran. murakumo is our first cluster (internal), not a product we are applying with. kotobase is the storage capability that deploy can grant, not a second applicant company; Terms still DRAFT, $0 verified external revenue. No Kotoba deploy revenue yet. We are not inventing ARR. We will not sell an effect (HTTP, storage, LLM) as production until that kit is qualified on the shipped backend.\n\nCategory: Developer Tools\n(Secondary if the form allows only one: Security.)\n\nOther ideas considered:\nWe are applying with one product: Kotoba language plus hosted deploy. murakumo is not another idea. It is the fleet we already deploy onto (33 compiled cores, internal). kotobase.net is a content-addressed GraphDB. In this company it is the storage capability the runtime can grant, not a separate YC product. Live site, Terms DRAFT, $0 verified external revenue. cloud-itonami is an industry operating system. Later it can be a distribution channel into real businesses. It is not this application. Live site, $0 verified external payment. If partners think the database or the industry OS is the better company, that is useful signal. The main answer is still language plus deploy.\n\n---\n\n## Equity\n\nHave you formed ANY legal entity yet?\nYes — related companies exist. The YC applicant company (Kotoba) is not formed yet.\n\nPlease list all legal entities:\n1. AWAI Network, L.L.C. — Delaware LLC, formed 2026-07-20 via Stripe Atlas, Delaware file 10704996. Sister operator for other products. Not the YC company.\n2. Gftd Japan K.K. — Japan, Corporate Number 1011101086505. Employment and some collection-agent work. Not the YC company.\n\nKotoba / Kotoba Lang, Inc. (planned Delaware C-Corp) will be formed after this application. Language, compiler, and runtime IP are intended to live there.\n\nDescribe the breakdown of equity ownership among the founders, employees, and any other shareholders.\nApplicant company not formed. Planned founder split once the C-Corp exists: Jun Kawasaki 90%, Ichihara 10%. No employees with equity. Sister companies are separate cap tables and are not this company.\n\nHave you taken any investment yet?\nNo, not for Kotoba.\n\nHow much money have you raised in total (USD)?\n0\n\nAre you currently fundraising?\nApplying to YC. Not otherwise fundraising.\n\n---\n\n## Curious\n\nWhat convinced you to apply to Y Combinator?\nThe compiler already runs our own production cores, and we still do not have a company a US buyer can write a deploy check to. YC is the shortest path to one company, one product (language plus hosted deploy), and a Delaware C-Corp that can take a SAFE. I am not applying because a market slide said to, and I am not applying with a pile of sister sites.\n\nHow did you hear about Y Combinator?\nLong-standing. OWNER-FILL if they want a first-contact story (HN, a specific batch company, an event).\n\n---\n\n## Owner remaining (do not skip)\n\n1. Record the 1-minute founder video. Upload unlisted YouTube. Paste URL.\n2. Fill founder profile: phone, education, LinkedIn, personal email.\n3. Write the non-computer systems hack in your own voice.\n4. Create/login at https://www.ycombinator.com/apply and paste.\n5. Form the Delaware C-Corp later. Do not delay submit for that.\n6. Ichihara must complete the founder profile at i@awai.network (submit is blocked until then). Known facts now filled: university senpai, 15+ years, company together at Jun age 20, Jun 90% / Ichihara 10%, Ichihara = marketing/sales/bizdev. Still needed: Ichihara profile, 1-minute video of both founders, Ichihara batch location, non-computer hack.\n\nDo not: claim sister-product users as Kotoba customers; claim $0 as unknown; use AWAI LLC as the YC company name; wait for counsel before submitting the application (incorporation is post-accept).\n"}]
